WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.907
Every AWS CLI command runs with an AWS
identity.

2
00:00:04.987 --> 00:00:10.936
That identity might be an IAM user, IAM
role, federated session,

3
00:00:11.016 --> 00:00:18.397
SSO session, instance role, container
role, workload identity, service role,

4
00:00:18.477 --> 00:00:19.799
or CI/CD role.

5
00:00:19.879 --> 00:00:26.930
The identity determines what the script
can read, create, update, publish,

6
00:00:27.010 --> 00:00:28.442
or administer.

7
00:00:28.522 --> 00:00:33.370
IAM design is therefore central to AWS CLI
security.

8
00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:36.645
Permissions should match the job.

9
00:00:36.725 --> 00:00:41.572
A reporting script should not use a
deployment role.

10
00:00:41.652 --> 00:00:48.923
A validation step should not need the same
permissions as a production change.

11
00:00:49.003 --> 00:00:56.384
A read-only inventory workflow should not
share access with an administrative

12
00:00:56.464 --> 00:00:57.456
workflow.

13
00:00:57.536 --> 00:01:04.366
Least privilege should consider service,
action, resource, condition,

14
00:01:04.446 --> 00:01:07.091
environment, and duration.

15
00:01:07.171 --> 00:01:13.230
The narrower the role, the easier it is to
understand and contain.

16
00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:18.378
Role assumption should be intentional and
auditable.

17
00:01:18.458 --> 00:01:25.619
The script should make clear which role it
expects to use and for what purpose.

18
00:01:25.699 --> 00:01:32.529
Teams should understand whether a role is
meant for read-only reporting,

19
00:01:32.609 --> 00:01:38.558
deployment, rollback, incident response,
or administration.

20
00:01:38.638 --> 00:01:46.350
Approval expectations and access
boundaries can help keep higher-risk roles from

21
00:01:46.430 --> 00:01:49.184
becoming ordinary defaults.

22
00:01:49.264 --> 00:01:55.764
Avoid broad shared administrator
credentials in AWS CLI automation.

23
00:01:55.844 --> 00:02:03.997
Shared high-privilege access makes
mistakes harder to attribute and incidents harder

24
00:02:04.077 --> 00:02:05.178
to contain.

25
00:02:05.258 --> 00:02:11.318
Each automation role should have an owner,
a documented purpose,

26
00:02:11.398 --> 00:02:15.584
a known usage location, and a review
process.

27
00:02:15.664 --> 00:02:23.045
Stale roles and unused permissions should
be cleaned up over time so automation

28
00:02:23.125 --> 00:02:26.100
access does not quietly expand.
